# continuation-licence.rq (loop O, 2026-08-03) — THE CONTINUATION LICENCE (residue R33).
#
# Open-world derivation (CLAUDE.md §8): "the pair (N-1, N) the recognition AXIOM licensed may
# actually be STITCHED" is GROWN from evidence both pages carry — the NON-TABLE text blocks the
# renderer drew around the table. Every input is a presence fact emitted by
# document.licence_evidence (one node per non-table block: its exact text, that same text with
# whole tokens equal to its page's printed ordinal cancelled, which page it sits on, and whether
# it sits ABOVE or BELOW that page's table), so this query carries NO numeric literal, no
# threshold, no tolerance — and never so much as a digit. A pair whose evidence does not
# meet the law stays UNLICENSED, and an unlicensed pair is two independent documents: recognition
# alone never stitches.
#
# WHY IT EXISTS. continuation-of.rq recognizes that page N redraws page N-1's header block on the
# same author grid. That is necessary but NOT sufficient for "one table" — two INDEPENDENT
# documents built from one TEMPLATE share exactly that evidence (measured, loop M task 2 review;
# residue R33, taxonomy case 2 vs case 3). The discriminator R33 names is the BODY side, and it is
# not text reading: a paginated table is CUT by the page edge, so what surrounds it either repeats
# on both pages (page furniture: running head, footer) or is new content — and new content at the
# CUT means a new document, not a continuation.
#
# READING vs COMPARING (the same distinction continuation-of.rq draws, and for the same reason —
# see its header's READING-vs-COMPARING block, which this query inherits verbatim in spirit). The
# law compares two renderings of the SAME string for literal identity. It draws no meaning from any
# string — no keyword, no "looks like a banner" judgment, and (see clause (b)) no notion of what a
# page number IS. The ordinal cancellation happens ENTIRELY AT EMISSION: this query compares two
# emitted strings for identity and never learns what an ordinal, a numeral or a token is; it does
# not contain a digit, a REPLACE, or any string surgery at all. Asking "did the renderer draw this
# block again on the other page?" is evidence comparison; asking "what does this block say?" would
# be reading, and would belong to NEURAL (§8).
#
# THE LAW — V4 (François's call, 2026-08-03, after V1-V3 were measured; see the task-2 report):
#   a recognized pair is licensed iff
#     (a) HEAD SIDE, STRICT — every non-table block ABOVE page N's table, other than the repeated
#         header block, has a TEXT-IDENTICAL counterpart block on page N-1; AND
#     (b) TAILS, INVARIANT MODULO THE PRINTED PAGE ORDINAL — every block BELOW EITHER page's table
#         has a counterpart on the other page whose text matches it EXCEPT for WHOLE TOKENS equal
#         to that block's OWN printed page ordinal (each side normalized by its own ordinal, then
#         compared for identity). Both directions: neither page's tail may carry a block the other
#         does not answer.
#   TOKENS MEANS TOKENS (round-2 review, F1): the unit is a whitespace-delimited token — the
#   repo's tokenization convention, the one rows._numeric_token_sum uses — so `1` cancels in
#   `... change 1` and does NOT cancel inside `141`, `11`, `1000` or `A1`. The normalization is
#   performed at emission by document._ordinal_normalized and reaches this query as a fact.
#   Blocks ABOVE page N-1's table are UNCONSTRAINED.
# THE ASYMMETRY IS LOAD-BEARING, and it is exactly the asymmetry of the CUT. The cut runs between
# page N-1's tail and page N's head, so those are the sides that must be furniture. What page N-1
# drew BEFORE its table says nothing about whether that table was cut: a document's opening
# furniture (its title, its print timestamp) is drawn ONCE, on the first page, and legitimately so
# — measured on the specimen, whose 'SHIPPING STEM' band and print timestamp are p0-only. A
# symmetric law would read that opening furniture as "page N-1 carries something page N does not"
# and refuse the GENUINE stitch.
# The answering side is unrestricted in both clauses: a constrained block may be answered by ANY
# block on the other page, wherever that one sits. Position decides whether a block must be
# ANSWERED, never what may answer it.
#
# WHY CLAUSE (b) IS MODULO THE ORDINAL — MEASURED, not assumed (specimen: the 3-page GrainCorp
# stem, corpus/ag-trade/graincorp-stem-2026-07-31.pdf, measured 2026-08-03).
# The stem is a GENUINE case-2 pagination, and a strictly-identical tail clause REFUSES both of its
# pairs. Its footer band (top=573.87 on all three pages, below the table on all three) reads
#   "GrainCorp advise that the load dates shown are indicative only and are subject to change 1"
#   "... 2"   "... 3"
# — the sentence is invariant and the PAGE NUMBER is not. That defeats a strict tail clause at
# EVERY block granularity (band, line, or word run: the numeral is its own word at x0=419.04, the
# sentence ending at x1=261.73), and it cannot be told apart, positionally, from the marked case-3
# banner this law exists to refuse — both are "same laid-out position, different text". So the
# difference the law reads is not position but WHAT the difference is: a whole token equal to the
# page's own printed ordinal, which the emitter cancels as a fact rather than the query
# recognizing a numeral. Head-side blocks stay STRICT precisely because that is where the marked
# case-3 evidence lives, and weakening them would give the false stitch back.
#
# WHAT THE NORMALIZATION DOES AND DOES NOT CANCEL (honest, §7 — measured at the token boundary,
# round-2 review; every line below is pinned by a probe in tests/etkl/test_continuation_licence):
#   * IT DOES NOT CANCEL A DIGIT INSIDE ANOTHER NUMBER, which is the whole reason the boundary
#     exists. `Subtotal 141` / `Subtotal 242` REFUSE, and so do `TOTAL 1000` / `TOTAL 2000` and
#     `Lot 11` / `Lot 22` — different numbers whose leading digit merely coincides with the page
#     ordinal, and precisely the below-table subtotal/total shape R33 must refuse. An earlier
#     substring form licensed all three (F1).
#   * IT DOES NOT CANCEL A DIGIT INSIDE A WORD-LIKE TOKEN: `Store A1 summary` / `Store A2
#     summary` REFUSE, because `A1` is one token and is not `1`. A per-page mark ATTACHED to
#     other characters is therefore content, not furniture.
#   * IT LICENSES THE COMMONEST REAL FOOTER IDIOM: `Page 1 of 12` / `Page 2 of 12` — `12` is not
#     the token `1`, so only the true ordinal cancels (the substring form refused this, F2).
#   * THE REMAINING PERMISSIVE CLASS, stated precisely because it is irreducible: two tails
#     differing ONLY by a STANDALONE token equal to each page's own ordinal, where that token is
#     not actually a page number — `Rev 1` / `Rev 2`, `Note 1` / `Note 2`. Nothing short of
#     reading the text can tell such a mark from a page number, and this law does not pretend to.
#     Registered under R33 rather than fixed.
#   * OVER-normalization (CONSERVATIVE, refuses): a STANDALONE ordinal token occurring elsewhere
#     in a tail is cancelled too, and each side cancels its OWN ordinal, so two genuinely
#     invariant tails carrying such a token normalize DIFFERENTLY (`Depot 1 line` under ordinal 1
#     vs the same string under ordinal 2) and the pair REFUSES. Refusal is the safe direction: an
#     unlicensed pair stays two independent documents.
#   * A document whose PRINTED ordinal differs from its page index (roman front matter, an offset
#     first page) simply fails to cancel, and the pair refuses — conservative again, and now
#     genuinely so: `Page 11` / `Page 22` under ordinals 1/2 REFUSE, where the substring form
#     breached the fail-safe and licensed them. The emitter states the assumption where it makes
#     it.
#
# VACUITY IS LICENCE, deliberately: a pair whose pages carry no non-table block at all (two bare
# tables, nothing around them) meets every clause trivially and is licensed. That is the
# fluent-reader invariant cutting the other way — two indistinguishable bare tables on consecutive
# pages ARE read as one continued table by a human reader, so stitching them is the correct
# reading, not a false positive. The law refuses on MARKS; absent marks it does not invent one.
#
# ROLE OF THE PAGE INDEX: none. The pair is chosen by the caller; the law reads only the two pages'
# block inventories. Nothing here counts pages or blocks, and no ordinal is ever compared for
# order — the ordinal never enters this query at all (tab:pageOrdinal is emitted for the audit
# trail, and this query does not read it).
PREFIX tab: <https://w3id.org/iladub/tab#>
SELECT ?pair WHERE {
  ?pair a tab:ContinuationPairUnderTest .
  # CLAUSE (a) — PAGE N'S HEAD CARRIES NOTHING NEW, STRICTLY. Every block the continuation page
  # drew ABOVE its table must be one the prior page also drew, character for character. The ?t
  # join IS the identity test (no normalisation, no fuzzy match), exactly as continuation-of.rq's
  # clause (b) joins on ?t. The repeated header block needs no exclusion of its own: it is drawn
  # INSIDE the recognized table band, and bands do not overlap, so no emitted block is it.
  FILTER NOT EXISTS {
    ?pair tab:hasPageBlock ?curHead .
    ?curHead a tab:ContinuationPageTextBlock .
    ?curHead a tab:AboveTableBlock .
    ?curHead tab:blockText ?t .
    FILTER NOT EXISTS {
      ?pair tab:hasPageBlock ?prior .
      ?prior a tab:PriorPageTextBlock .
      ?prior tab:blockText ?t .
    }
  }
  # CLAUSE (b1) — PAGE N-1's TAIL IS FURNITURE, NOT CONTENT. Whatever the prior page drew BELOW
  # its table must reappear on page N, up to that block's own printed ordinal; if it does not,
  # that page's table was not cut by the page edge — something else followed it, and the pages are
  # two documents. Each side arrives ALREADY normalized by its own ordinal (at emission, whole
  # tokens only), so the ?n join IS the comparison — the same identity-by-join idiom clause (a)
  # uses on ?t, and no numeral, digit or string function enters this query.
  FILTER NOT EXISTS {
    ?pair tab:hasPageBlock ?priorTail .
    ?priorTail a tab:PriorPageTextBlock .
    ?priorTail a tab:BelowTableBlock .
    ?priorTail tab:ordinalNormalizedText ?n .
    FILTER NOT EXISTS {
      ?pair tab:hasPageBlock ?answer .
      ?answer a tab:ContinuationPageTextBlock .
      ?answer tab:ordinalNormalizedText ?n .
    }
  }
  # CLAUSE (b2) — THE MIRROR, so neither page's tail may carry a block the other does not answer.
  FILTER NOT EXISTS {
    ?pair tab:hasPageBlock ?curTail .
    ?curTail a tab:ContinuationPageTextBlock .
    ?curTail a tab:BelowTableBlock .
    ?curTail tab:ordinalNormalizedText ?n2 .
    FILTER NOT EXISTS {
      ?pair tab:hasPageBlock ?answer2 .
      ?answer2 a tab:PriorPageTextBlock .
      ?answer2 tab:ordinalNormalizedText ?n2 .
    }
  }
}
